{{- /*
  The single entry point for "what the VS Code User settings.json contains
  for this user". Consumers (both OS targets -
  Library/Application Support/Code/User/settings.json.tmpl and
  .config/Code/User/settings.json.tmpl, see .chezmoiignore for how each
  machine only gets its own one) call ONLY this and get back the final JSON -
  they never need to know anything about the per-user overlay, which is
  entirely an authoring-time concern here and in the data files.

  Composes:
  - .chezmoitemplates/vscode-settings.json - the shared base, PURE JSON,
    parsed with `fromJson` (not `fromYaml`), so it must stay free of both
    template syntax and comments;
  - an optional .chezmoidata/vscode-<username>.yaml overlay (key
    per_user_<username>, same convention as packages-<username>.yaml):
    `settings` merged over the base with the user winning per key, `remove`
    listing base keys to delete outright.
    The merge is HAND-ROLLED, not sprig's `merge` - confirmed the hard way
    (same lesson as .chezmoitemplates/merged-packages): sprig's merge
    silently drops "zero" values, so a user's `false`/`[]`/`0` override
    gets replaced by the base's non-empty value (redhat.telemetry.enabled:
    false came out as the base's true) and the base's null-valued key
    (hediet.vscode-drawio.resizeImages) vanished from everyone's output.
    Here: every user key wins unconditionally, then base keys fill in only
    where genuinely absent (hasKey), so false/[]/null survive on both sides.
    Shallow merge: an overlay key replaces the base key wholesale, so
    nested objects in an overlay must be complete.
  A user with no overlay file gets the base as-is (nil -> dict, merged with
  nothing). `index`, not dot-notation, for every possibly-missing key - dot
  errors on a map that lacks the key entirely, `index` returns nil.

  Output is `toPrettyJson`, which alphabetizes keys and re-indents: the
  rendered settings.json keeps every value but is reformatted once, on the
  next apply, for every user - cosmetic only (VS Code rewrites this file
  itself whenever a setting changes, and JSON key order has no meaning).
  Was previously synced via VS Code's own Settings Sync (Microsoft/GitHub
  account) - moved to chezmoi instead, for privacy (no third-party account
  holding it) and so update.mode can actually stick without Sync
  overwriting it. If you re-enable Settings Sync on any machine, it'll
  fight this file the same way Rancher Desktop fought ~/.bashrc (see
  modify_dot_bashrc.tmpl) - keep it off everywhere this is applied.
*/ -}}
{{- $base := include ".chezmoitemplates/vscode-settings.json" | fromJson -}}
{{- $perUser := index . (printf "per_user_%s" .chezmoi.username) -}}
{{- if not $perUser }}{{ $perUser = dict }}{{ end -}}
{{- $merged := dict -}}
{{- range $k, $v := default dict (index $perUser "settings") -}}
{{- $_ := set $merged $k $v -}}
{{- end -}}
{{- range $k, $v := $base -}}
{{- if not (hasKey $merged $k) -}}{{- $_ := set $merged $k $v -}}{{- end -}}
{{- end -}}
{{- range $key := default list (index $perUser "remove") -}}
{{- $_ := unset $merged $key -}}
{{- end -}}
{{- $merged | toPrettyJson }}{{ "\n" }}
